            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Analyzing confidence reports**\n\nI’m working through some odds calculations, aiming to understand how confidence can reflect strength in assessments. It seems that different confidence levels indicate varying likelihoods, like with B scoring .771 suggesting three recommendations and B at .6 possibly indicating two. I’m wondering if we can treat confidence as informative thresholds without assuming it’s absolute truth. We might need to focus on unique surveys from peers without double counting the reports. It feels a bit complex, but I think there's clarity in how we approach it.**Mapping confidence patterns**\n\nI'm trying to analyze confidence levels based on values from different analysts. I've noticed both A and B share similar confidence scores, which seems strange. If B has higher confidence values, it makes me question how those correspond to their surveys. Shouldn't A have a lower score when it only has one high recommendation? I'm considering treating each forecast as a noisy observation and aggregating the log odds from reports. Also, I need to remember that stated confidence doesn't guarantee accuracy!**Understanding analyst reports and confidence**\n\nI've been thinking about how analysts' reports rely on distinct survey observations. It seems important to count each observation only once, rather than per recommendation. I wonder if confidence is just redundant metadata and shouldn't be taken as strong evidence. In the context of Bayesian aggregation, confidence might reflect beliefs rather than exact surveys. The note about confidence being an analyst's probability of accuracy reminds me not to confuse confidence with truth but to treat it as a probability indicator instead.",
